{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>But no man shall sanctify it</b> — for the name of another offering, because it is not his, this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 27:26). But the correct interpretation is that it means there is no need to sanctify it, for if it is an ox or a sheep, it belongs to HaShem of itself. And it is like something preemptively removed. And in the Mishneh Torah it is stated: \"All the firstborn that is born in your herd or in your flock, the male you shall sanctify to HaShem your Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 15:19) — that it should be holy and treated with sanctity, that you shall not work with it and not shear it, and you shall eat it before HaShem, as he will explain."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The phrase והנה הוא כמו שלילות is translated hyper-literally; the term שלילות in this context is unusual and may carry a technical halakhic or Aramaic connotation rendered here as 'preemptively removed.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:01:23.836925196Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}